How It Works:
Iterative Traversal:
Start from the root.
While the current node (root) is not NULL:
No Left Child: If root has no left child, move to the right child.
With Left Child:
Find the rightmost node (curr) in the left subtree.
Connect the original right subtree to curr->right.
Move the left subtree to the right (root->right = root->left).
Set root->left to NULL.
Move to the right child.
Time and Space Complexity:
Time Complexity: O(N)
Each node is visited at most twice (once to find the rightmost node and once to flatten).
Space Complexity: O(1)
The operation is done in-place without using additional memory.


Code Snippet:
void flatten(Node *root)
{
    while(root){
        if(!root->left) {
            root = root->right;
        }
        else{
            Node* curr = root->left;
            while(curr->right){
                curr = curr->right;
            }
            curr->right = root->right;
            root->right = root->left;
            root->left = NULL;
            root = root->right;
        }
    }
}


Example:
Given the following binary tree:


    1
   / \
  2   5
 / \   \
3   4   6
After calling flatten(root), the tree becomes:


1
 \
 2
  \
   3
    \
     4
      \
       5
        \
         6
This function efficiently flattens the tree in linear time with constant space, making it optimal for scenarios where memory usage is a constraint.
